// "<creature> fights <creature>" [CR#701.14a]: "a spell or ability may
// instruct a creature to fight another creature", and each deals damage equal
// to its power to the other. The rules instruct the first creature, so a card
// hands the action to it (`act(target(creature), fight(target(creature)))`)
// and the wrapper records that permanent; inside the handoff `actor` is the
// fighting permanent, and the other creature is the parameter. Written with
// no handoff, the source permanent fights. The other creature is mentioned
// three times, so it is bound where the first damage event first mentions it
// and read back by identity in the rest (`deckmaste_semantics_v2::mentions`):
// a target is selected once. The rule's own wording, "each of those creatures
// deals damage equal to its power to the other creature", is a reciprocal the
// model lacks; the body writes the two damage events instead (the gap was
// routed to `semantics-v2-anaphor-resolution-heuristics`, now done; it lives
// on in `lean-drt-anaphora-refactor`). That a creature no longer on the
// battlefield deals no fight damage [CR#701.14b] is the rules' to apply, not
// a guard here: a guard's condition may not introduce its subject.
KeywordAction(
    name: "fight",
    params: [Subject],
    spelling: "fight <Param(0)>",
    grammar: Verb(
        bare: "fight",
        frame_set: Transitive,
    ),
    body: simultaneously([
        dealDamage(actor, powerOf(actor), Param(0)),
        dealDamage(Param(0), powerOf(Param(0)), actor),
    ]),
)
